<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Auferstanden aus Ruinen&quot; (&quot;Risen from Ruins&quot;) was the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>. In 1949 the Soviet occupation zone of Allied-occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> became a socialist state, the German Democratic Republic (GDR). For its anthem the poet Johannes Becher, who later became the East German Minister of Culture, wrote the words. Two musicians, Ottmar Gerster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanns-eisler">Hanns Eisler</a>, proposed settings, and Eisler's was chosen.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Auferstanden aus Ruinen</b>&quot; (&quot;Risen from Ruins&quot;) was the national anthem of East Germany.</blockquote><a href="/songs/auferstanden-aus-ruinen">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</a></li><li><blockquote>East Germany had its own anthem, &quot;<b>Auferstanden aus Ruinen</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a></li><li><blockquote>Eisler, who later composed the East German anthem &quot;<b>Auferstanden aus Ruinen</b>&quot;, kept the setting deliberately simple so that workers without musical training could sing it; the result is close to a march.</blockquote><a href="/songs/einheitsfrontlied">Einheitsfrontlied</a></li></ul></section>
